.TH GROUPER 1 "July 2026" "sumtag 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
grouper \- find groups of identical or similar directories in a sumtag database
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B grouper
.BI \-\-database " DB"
.RI [ ACTIONS ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B grouper
finds groups of directories whose contents are identical or nearly so \(em
for example, two slightly different versions of the same project saved at
slightly different times. It is an experimental companion to
.BR sumtag (1):
it works entirely from a sumtag SQLite database (see the
.B \-\-database
option of
.BR sumtag (1)),
never reading file contents or extended attributes and never computing a
checksum itself. The database must have been populated by sumtag first.
.PP
Alongside sumtag's own tables, grouper owns a set of derived tables inside
the same database file
.RB ( dirs ", " dir_files ", " dir_pairs ", " groups ", " group_dirs ", "
.BR grouper_meta ).
All of them are rebuildable from sumtag's tables, and all can be dropped
with
.BR \-\-clean\-db .
.PP
The work is a pipeline of three explicit stages, each persisted so later
stages are cheap to re-run:
.IP 1. 3
.B \-\-index
distills the file rows into the distinct directories that directly contain
at least one stamped file. Only direct children count \(em a directory's own
file listing is its signature.
.IP 2. 3
.B \-\-pairs
compares every directory to every other with one named comparison function
(see
.B COMPARISON FUNCTIONS
below) and stores every nonzero similarity. This is the expensive stage, so
the similarity threshold is deliberately not applied here: regrouping at a
different threshold recomputes nothing.
.IP 3. 3
.B \-\-threshold
walks the stored pairs best-first and partitions the directories into
groups \(em one placement decision per directory, and groups are never
merged. At equal similarity, the pair sharing more files ranks first, so
large overlaps seed groups ahead of trivial ones. The result is persisted
and reported.
.PP
.B \-\-prep
runs stages 1 and 2 together. A bare
.B grouper \-\-database
.I DB
with no action reports the stored grouping again. Stages may be combined in
one invocation
.RB ( \-\-prep " " \-\-threshold " 0.7)."
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-database " DB"
Path to the sumtag SQLite database. Required on every invocation.
.TP
.B \-\-prep
Stages 1+2:
.B \-\-index
then
.B \-\-pairs
in one shot.
.TP
.B \-\-index
Stage 1: (re)build the directory index. Silent on success.
.TP
.B \-\-pairs
Stage 2: compare every directory to every other and store all nonzero
similarities. Silent on success. Scoring fans out across
.B \-\-jobs
worker processes; every database write stays in the parent. The stored pair
set is identical at any
.B \-\-jobs
value.
.TP
.BI \-\-threshold " X"
Stage 3: build and persist the grouping from the stored pairs, using
.I X
(0.0\-1.0) as the similar-enough bar, then report it. Skipped when the
stored grouping is already current for this threshold and comparison
function \(em in that case it just reports. A threshold below a stored
.B \-\-min\-sim
floor is refused with a hint to re-run
.BR \-\-pairs ,
never answered silently from an incomplete pair table.
.TP
.BI \-\-fn " FN"
Comparison function for
.BR \-\-pairs / \-\-compare
(see
.B COMPARISON FUNCTIONS
below). Default:
.BR name\-score .
.TP
.BI \-\-jobs " N"
Worker processes for
.B \-\-pairs
scoring (default: all CPUs); 1 disables multiprocessing. Each worker loads
its own signature table from the database, so every worker holds a full
copy in memory \(em on a large corpus this option is a memory knob as much
as a speed knob. Small corpora run serially regardless.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-df " N"
For
.BR \-\-pairs :
instead of exhaustive all-pairs comparison, score only candidate pairs that
share at least one signature token (a basename or digest, depending on the
comparison function) present in at most
.I N
directories. Candidates get exact scores from full signatures \(em the cap
governs who gets looked at, never what a look sees; what is given up is
only pairs whose sole overlap is ubiquitous tokens, whose similarity is
necessarily tiny. By default, comparison is exhaustive up to roughly ten
thousand directories, beyond which candidate mode engages with
.IR N =1000;
0 forces exhaustive at any size. Candidate mode is announced on standard
error and the cap is recorded in the database.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-sim " X"
For
.BR \-\-pairs :
keep only pairs scoring at least
.I X
(0.0\-1.0). By default every nonzero pair is stored so that
.B \-\-threshold
stays a free exploratory knob, but at archive scale the weak tail dwarfs
everything interesting. The floor governs what is kept, never what is
looked at; scores are still computed exactly. A later
.B \-\-threshold
below the floor is refused (see above).
.TP
.B \-\-name
For
.BR \-\-pairs / \-\-compare :
two directories whose basenames differ score 0, unconditionally \(em only
same-named directories can pair, and therefore group. The typical prey is
versioned copies of one tree
.RB ( proj /,
.BR backup/proj /,
.BR old/proj /),
where the directory's own name survives a copy even as contents drift. Also
sharply reduces what
.B \-\-pairs
stores.
.TP
.B \-\-progress
Live progress bar on standard error for the build stages
.RB ( \-\-index / \-\-pairs ),
following
.BR sumtag (1)'s
.B \-\-progress
conventions: appears once a stage has run two seconds, redrawn in place,
cleared on completion, suppressed when standard error is not a terminal.
Shows done/total with rate, percent, elapsed time, and an ETA.
.TP
.BI \-\-ls " DIR"
List the files in
.I DIR
(absolute or mount-relative) using the directory index.
.TP
.BI \-\-compare " DIR_A DIR_B"
Print the similarity (0.0\-1.0) of two directories' contents.
.TP
.B \-\-dups
Report duplicate files (same digest).
.TP
\fB\-\-top\fR [\fIN\fR]
Report the
.I N
(default 1) most frequently occurring checksums and their files, excluding
empty files. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-dups .
.TP
.BI \-\-min " N"
For
.BR \-\-dups / \-\-top :
only report groups of at least
.I N
files (default: 2).
.TP
.B \-\-clean\-db
Drop every grouper-owned derived table and
.BR VACUUM ,
returning their space \(em
.B dir_pairs
in particular can dwarf the sumtag tables it was derived from. All-or-nothing
on purpose: the artifacts are interdependent, so a partial clean would leave
a grouping that cannot be reported. Sumtag's own tables are never touched.
Cannot be combined with any other action.
.SH COMPARISON FUNCTIONS
Similarity is size-normalised: two identical 10,000-file archives and two
identical 3-file folders both score 1.0. Directories with no signature
overlap always score 0.
.TP
.B digest
Content only; a renamed file still matches.
.TP
.B name\-digest
All-or-nothing per (name, digest) pair: a file counts only when both its
basename and its digest agree.
.TP
.B name\-score
The default. Name-anchored partial credit: one point for a shared basename,
two more if the digests also agree.
.SH INTERRUPTION
An interrupted
.B \-\-pairs
run keeps its completed inserts, but the run is not marked finished: a
partial pair table can never masquerade as a complete one, and
.B \-\-threshold
refuses it until a
.B \-\-pairs
run completes.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success;
.B 1
when an error prevents the request from being answered (no stored pairs, a
threshold below the stored floor, an unknown directory, and so on). Errors
are reported on standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Build everything and group at 0.7:
.PP
.EX
grouper \-\-database foo.sqlite \-\-prep \-\-threshold 0.7
.EE
.PP
Regroup at a looser threshold (recomputes nothing):
.PP
.EX
grouper \-\-database foo.sqlite \-\-threshold 0.5
.EE
.PP
Report the stored grouping again:
.PP
.EX
grouper \-\-database foo.sqlite
.EE
.PP
Compare two directories:
.PP
.EX
grouper \-\-database foo.sqlite \-\-compare /data/proj /backup/proj
.EE
.PP
Prepare a very large corpus, keeping only meaningful pairs between
same-named directories:
.PP
.EX
grouper \-\-database big.sqlite \-\-prep \-\-name \-\-min\-sim 0.4 \-\-progress
.EE
.PP
Return the space the derived tables occupy:
.PP
.EX
grouper \-\-database foo.sqlite \-\-clean\-db
.EE
.SH NOTES
.B grouper
is experimental. Its derived tables go stale when sumtag rescans the
filesystem; re-run the pipeline
.RB ( \-\-prep )
to refresh them. Duplicate detection is scoped to a single digest algorithm
at a time: two identical files stamped under different algorithms are not
found as duplicates.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sumtag (1),
.BR dedupe (1)
.SH AUTHOR
The sumtag authors.
